.TH who "" "Command"
.SH NAME
who \- print who is logged in
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwho\fR
.br
\fBwho \fIwho-file\fR
.br
\fBwho \fBam i\fR
.br
\fBwho \fIwho-file \fBam i\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
who -- print who is logged in
Usage:	who [ file ] [ am i ]
.HE
With no arguments,
.B who
reads
.B /etc/utmp
and prints one line for each user currently logged in, giving the
login name, terminal line, and login time; entries with no login name
(unused slots) are skipped.
.PP
If a
.I who-file
is named,
.B who
reads that file instead of
.BR /etc/utmp ,
and prints every entry found in it, including unused entries.
This is useful, for example, with
.BR /usr/adm/wtmp ,
which keeps a running record of logins and logouts.
.PP
If the literal words
.B am
and
.B i
follow (as the last two arguments),
.B who
prints only the entry whose terminal line matches the invoker's own
terminal.
.PP
Each line has the form
.DS
.ta 10n 18n
\fIname\fR	\fIline\fR	\fImon day hh:mm\fR
.DE
where
.I name
and
.I line
come from the utmp entry and the date and time are taken from
.BR ctime() ,
with the day-of-week, seconds, and year dropped.
.SH FILES
.B /etc/utmp
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR write ,
.BR login ,
.B mesg
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On an unsupported combination of arguments,
.B who
prints (prefixed with
.B "who: "
unless standard output is a terminal)
.B "Usage: who [who-file] [am I]"
and exits with status 1; the same routine reports
.B "Cannot open who-file"
if the named, or default, file cannot be opened.
